# weapons design rows: Rome hand weapons as designer data (Bycer ch3 ch8 tells and commit; Sellers ch9-10 resource and gates).
# Designers change numbers here without a Rust edit; the game test in
# crates/game/tests/it/design_combat.rs checks them. Whole numbers only,
# `#` comments, no floats. Units live in the key: `_permille` is parts per
# thousand, `_ticks` is fixed ticks at 60 Hz. Stamina costs are points,
# hands and counter and type are plain counts.
# Columns: type (1 thrust, 2 cut, 3 throw, 4 blow), damage_permille, speed_ticks
# (startup ticks, fewer is faster), hands (1 or 2), stagger_permille (poise
# hold 0-1000, higher holds longer), stamina_cost (points, heavy above
# light), counter (1 parry, 2 dodge, 3 block: the answer from the player's
# own kit that beats this weapon).
# v2 adds the frame-data fields of docs/design/FORMAT.md: active_ticks (live
# ticks; steel hits only here), recovery_ticks (committed ticks after active),
# avoid (parry, dodge, block or range) and punish_ticks (the window to hit
# back; it sits inside recovery). Every v1 number is unchanged.
# The type says what the weapon does: the gladius thrusts (Vegetius 1.12),
# the pilum is thrown and so it is one hand, not a two-hand thrust.
# House numbers: readable startups sit in 24-48 ticks (dagger 24, gladius 30
# matches v2 startup, spatha 36, pilum 48 heavy windup; OPT59 doubled the old
# 12-24 so the 30-tick readable tell holds); fast weapons hit for less; every
# weapon has one counter, and the steel stays live 2-3 ticks.
# FEEL1 adds mass_g (grams, docs/plan/melee-feel.md): the live duel arms a row per
# fighter, so a heavier weapon starts later, recovers longer and staggers harder;
# sorted by mass, startup, recovery and stagger never fall (combat heft test).
# The gladius row equals the shipped v2 table (startup 30, recovery 6), so arming
# it changes no bout.
# BG15 adds edge_wear_permille (0-1000, parts of a fully dulled edge): the
# condition the kit's steel arrives with. The cut resolver scales a landed
# blow's energy by `1 - wear`, so a dulled gladius severs less than a fresh
# one; a row that names no wear is a fresh edge. A blunt weapon has no edge to
# dull, so it names 0. Book note: Roman campaign steel came to the field
# already used — a legionary sharpened on the march, not before every bout.
# Book notes: weapons as data rows `[handsrust | Damage from Weapons and Claws]`.
table weapons version 2
pugio type=1 damage_permille=80 speed_ticks=24 hands=1 stagger_permille=300 stamina_cost=10 counter=2 avoid=dodge active_ticks=2 recovery_ticks=6 punish_ticks=4 mass_g=300 edge_wear_permille=80
gladius type=1 damage_permille=120 speed_ticks=30 hands=1 stagger_permille=400 stamina_cost=15 counter=1 avoid=parry active_ticks=2 recovery_ticks=6 punish_ticks=6 mass_g=1000 edge_wear_permille=120
spatha type=2 damage_permille=150 speed_ticks=36 hands=1 stagger_permille=500 stamina_cost=20 counter=3 avoid=block active_ticks=3 recovery_ticks=10 punish_ticks=8 mass_g=1300 edge_wear_permille=90
clava type=4 damage_permille=165 speed_ticks=42 hands=1 stagger_permille=550 stamina_cost=25 counter=3 avoid=block active_ticks=3 recovery_ticks=12 punish_ticks=9 mass_g=1700 edge_wear_permille=0
pilum type=3 damage_permille=180 speed_ticks=48 hands=1 stagger_permille=600 stamina_cost=30 counter=2 avoid=dodge active_ticks=3 recovery_ticks=14 punish_ticks=10 mass_g=2100 edge_wear_permille=150
